Stage 7: the same arms under BenchmarkDotNet 0.15.8, the harness the CONTROLLED
RERUN should use.

commit:   1e3936a134732ee08788c1624317839c25f703eb
machine:  4 vCPU Intel(R) Xeon(R) Processor @ 2.80GHz, 15 GiB, 6.18.44-fc-v37
sdk:      8.0.131;  Google.Protobuf 3.28.3, Grpc.Tools 2.66.0

RE-TAKEN after JOURNAL.md entry 14. The previous stage 7 log is WITHDRAWN: it
was taken with the two handicapped incumbent arms, so its decode column was
flattered by 0.10 to 0.18 and its encode baseline by 20 to 29 percent.
  * gp-bufferwriter is new: WriteTo(IBufferWriter<byte>) over a reused BufWriter,
    the incumbent's fastest official encode path, with NO top-level size pass.
    The baseline column stays gp-writeto so the Ratio is comparable with the
    hand-rolled harness, but a VERDICT should be read against gp-bufferwriter.
  * both decode arms now park the decoded graph in a static sink and return an
    O(1) value. The old gp-parse ended with m.CalculateSize(), a full traversal
    of the decoded tree that managed-parse did not pay.

144 benchmarks: 16 payloads x 6 encode arms + 16 x 3 decode arms.
BenchmarkDotNet defaults, each benchmark in its own process.

It subtracts its own overhead, iterates warmup to a convergence criterion,
reports a 99.9% CI and removes outliers, and adds Gen0/1/2 counts. What it does
NOT do is interleave: per-benchmark isolation is the opposite of R4, and on a
shared container an isolated arm can land in a quiet minute while another lands
in a busy one. On a controlled machine that noise is gone and the isolation is
the better choice, which is why this is the harness to hand the rerun. Here,
the two harnesses agreeing is what makes either trustworthy.

